(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-valvular-heart-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Valvular Heart Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which valve lesion goes with a Corrigan pulse?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A Corrigan pulse, the water hammer or collapsing pulse, has a very rapid upstroke followed by a rapid collapse. It goes with chronic aortic regurgitation, and a question may name it without describing it. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which regurgitant fraction marks severe mitral regurgitation?","choices":["20% or more","40% or more","50% or more","30% or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe mitral regurgitation is a regurgitant fraction of 50% or more, half of the stroke volume going back into the left atrium. Moderate is 30 to 49% and mild under 30%. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which event produces the first heart sound (S1)?","choices":["Aortic and pulmonic valves closing","Rapid filling of a dilated ventricle","Atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle","Mitral and tricuspid valves closing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The first heart sound is the atrioventricular valves, mitral and tricuspid, closing as the ventricles begin to contract. It marks the start of systole. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which transcatheter device repairs a leaking mitral valve without replacing it?","choices":["Tricuspid annuloplasty ring","A mitral valve clip","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","Transcatheter aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mitral valve clip grasps the leaflets to reduce the leak. The other surgical options for mitral regurgitation are transcatheter mitral valve replacement or a mechanical or biological valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram findings fit pulmonic stenosis?","choices":["Right axis deviation, right ventricular hypertrophy","Left axis deviation, left ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial abnormality, atrial fibrillation","Right atrial enlargement, no ventricular change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis raises right ventricular pressure, and the electrocardiogram shows right axis deviation with right ventricular hypertrophy. The echo shows abnormal valve motion and a raised jet on Doppler. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which drug is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist for a mechanical valve?","choices":["Aspirin","Clopidogrel","Warfarin","Heparin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, the long-term anticoagulant for mechanical heart valves; if surgery requires heparin instead, warfarin is bridged until the ratio is therapeutic. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram finding points to tricuspid stenosis?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy out of proportion to the left atrium","Right atrial enlargement out of proportion to the right ventricle","Left atrial enlargement out of proportion to the left ventricle","Right ventricular hypertrophy out of proportion to the right atrium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis shows right atrial enlargement out of proportion to right ventricular hypertrophy, because the obstruction sits behind the right atrium and spares the ventricle. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Tricuspid stenosis obstructs flow between which chambers?","choices":["Right atrium to right ventricle","Left atrium to left ventricle","Right ventricle to pulmonary artery","Left ventricle to aorta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis narrows the valve between the right atrium and right ventricle, obstructing inflow; the right atrium dilates and hypertrophies, with low cardiac output and portal congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 65, 66"},{"q":"Which ventricle does a fourth heart sound (S4) point to?","choices":["A thin, dilated right ventricle","A thick, hypertrophied left ventricle","A dilated, volume-loaded left ventricle","A normal ventricle closing its valves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The fourth heart sound is the high-pressure sound of diastolic heart failure: left ventricular hypertrophy, as from aortic stenosis, with the atrium contracting against a stiff ventricle. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the management approach to pulmonic regurgitation?","choices":["Tricuspid annuloplasty","Treat the underlying condition","Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Lifelong anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is managed by treating the underlying condition, with referral when it is seen on an echocardiogram or when right heart failure symptoms and signs appear. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"For tricuspid stenosis, what is balloon valvotomy reserved for?","choices":["Every patient with a murmur","Mild disease without symptoms","Children and young adults only","Hepatic congestion risking cirrhosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Balloon valvotomy and valve repair are reserved for tricuspid stenosis with hepatic congestion that may lead to cirrhosis, or severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the treatment for acute severe aortic regurgitation?","choices":["Watchful waiting with repeat echo","Emergency valve replacement or repair","Afterload reduction with hydralazine","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute severe aortic regurgitation is treated with emergency aortic valve replacement or repair, ideally within 24 hours; the patient presents in cardiogenic shock with pulmonary edema. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 26, 31"},{"q":"Which change makes the pulmonic regurgitation murmur louder?","choices":["Expiration","Sitting upright","Squatting","Standing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases venous return makes the pulmonic regurgitation murmur louder: inspiration, squatting and lying supine. Expiration and standing, which reduce venous return, soften it. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Pulmonic stenosis is commonly part of which congenital condition?","choices":["Ankylosing spondylitis","Tetralogy of Fallot","Marfan syndrome","A bicuspid aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis is commonly part of tetralogy of Fallot. It is almost always congenital, and carcinoid disease is the other listed cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why is rheumatic mitral stenosis rare in developed countries?","choices":["Degenerative disease has replaced it","Bicuspid valves are repaired at birth","Balloon valvotomy prevents narrowing","Rheumatic fever has become less common"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis is rare in developed countries because the incidence of rheumatic fever, its most common cause, has fallen there. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What treatment does uncomplicated mitral valve prolapse usually need?","choices":["Usually none","Lifelong anticoagulation","Early valve replacement","Balloon valvotomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse usually needs no treatment. If it progresses to mitral regurgitation, the options are a clip, transcatheter mitral valve replacement, or a biological or mechanical valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which study should any patient with symptoms suggestive of aortic stenosis receive?","choices":["A chest radiograph","An electrocardiogram alone","An echocardiogram","A left heart catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional chest pressure, shortness of breath or syncope suggestive of aortic stenosis calls for an echocardiogram, which shows thickened, calcified leaflets with reduced opening and grades severity. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the usual course of mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["Rapidly fatal within years","Relentlessly progressive stenosis","Heart failure by middle age","Benign and asymptomatic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is usually benign and asymptomatic, needing no treatment unless it progresses to mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which symptoms appear in severe tricuspid regurgitation?","choices":["Angina and exertional syncope","Head bobbing and wide pulses","Pedal edema and ascites","Hemoptysis and hoarseness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate tricuspid regurgitation is usually asymptomatic; severe disease causes pedal edema and ascites as systemic venous pressure rises. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Primary tricuspid regurgitation from infective endocarditis is typical of whom?","choices":["Intravenous drug users","Healthy women aged 15 to 30","Adults older than 70","Children with tetralogy of Fallot"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary tricuspid regurgitation comes from infective endocarditis in intravenous drug users. Endocarditis from other sources more often involves the mitral valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which valve lies beneath the second intercostal space at the left sternal border?","choices":["Aortic","Tricuspid","Mitral","Pulmonic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonic area is the second intercostal space at the left sternal border. Questions give the location in these anatomic terms rather than naming the area, so the valve underneath has to be known. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which advice fits a patient with aortic stenosis?","choices":["Start high-intensity interval training","Carry a nitrate for exertional chest pain","Avoid strenuous activity and competitive sports","Restrict fluids to lower cardiac workload"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medical care for mild and moderate aortic stenosis rests on the preload-dependent physiology: avoid strenuous physical activity and competitive sports, and avoid dehydration and hypovolemia. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of pulmonic regurgitation?","choices":["Iatrogenic injury","Pulmonary hypertension","Carcinoid disease","A bicuspid valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of pulmonic regurgitation is iatrogenic: surgical valvotomy, valvectomy or valvuloplasty done to relieve right ventricular outflow tract obstruction leaves the valve leaking. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In mitral stenosis, atrial fibrillation is a risk factor for what?","choices":["Papillary muscle rupture","Thromboembolism","Aortic dissection","Rheumatic fever recurrence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Left atrial enlargement predisposes to atrial fibrillation, and atrial fibrillation is a risk factor for thromboembolism, one of the main dangers of mitral stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the target international normalized ratio for a mechanical aortic valve?","choices":["2.5 (range 2.0 to 3.0)","3.0 (range 2.5 to 3.5)","1.5 (range 1.0 to 2.0)","4.0 (range 3.5 to 4.5)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical aortic valve targets an international normalized ratio of 2.5, range 2.0 to 3.0. A mechanical mitral valve, or valves in both positions, targets 3.0. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which is avoided as the sole long-term therapy for a mechanical heart valve?","choices":["A vitamin K antagonist","Warfarin","Warfarin with a heparin bridge for surgery","An antiplatelet agent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over antiplatelet agents for the long-term management of mechanical valves, so an antiplatelet agent alone is not adequate. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Who accounts for most mitral stenosis caused by rheumatic fever?","choices":["Men, about 80%","Children under 10","Adults older than 70","Women, about 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 80% of mitral stenosis due to rheumatic fever occurs in women. By contrast, 80% of patients with symptomatic aortic stenosis are men. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Who is the typical patient with mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["A healthy woman aged 15 to 30","A man older than 70","A person who injects drugs","A child with tetralogy of Fallot"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is usually found in healthy women 15 to 30 years old, is usually benign and asymptomatic, and is heard as a mid-systolic click at the apex. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which drug relieves dyspnea and orthopnea in mitral stenosis?","choices":["A loop diuretic","A nitrate","Hydralazine","Warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of dyspnea and orthopnea in mitral stenosis are treated with loop diuretics such as furosemide or bumetanide, which relieve the pulmonary congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which procedure treats tricuspid regurgitation caused by annular dilation?","choices":["A mitral valve clip","Annuloplasty","Valve replacement","Balloon valvotomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation from annular dilation is treated with annuloplasty after treating the underlying condition; valve repair or replacement is used when the cause is primary. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"}]};
